window.docContents[198].resolve({"/Lecture-1___-Motivation-and-Propositional-Logic/Natural-Deduction/#Formal-Software-Verification--Lecture-1___-Motivation-and-Propositional-Logic--Natural-Deduction--Negation-and-Falsehood":{"contents":"The constant ⊥ is the absurdity, the proposition with no introduction rule. Negation abbreviates ¬P as P → ⊥, so the rules for negation are the implication rules read at ⊥. To introduce ¬P, assume P, derive ⊥, and discharge the assumption. To eliminate it, a proof of P and a proof of ¬P together yield ⊥. From ⊥, elimination proves any proposition C, the principle ex falso quodlibet.\n\n   [P]\n    ⋮\n    ⊥                  P    ¬P               ⊥\n  ───────  ¬I         ─────────  ¬E        ─────  ⊥E\n    ¬P                    ⊥                   C\n\n\n","context":"Formal Software Verification\u0009Lecture 1: Motivation and Propositional Logic\u0009Natural Deduction","header":"1.5.4. Negation and Falsehood","id":"/Lecture-1___-Motivation-and-Propositional-Logic/Natural-Deduction/#Formal-Software-Verification--Lecture-1___-Motivation-and-Propositional-Logic--Natural-Deduction--Negation-and-Falsehood"}});